  {
   "id": "AiTChQ-pAAAJ",
   "from": "Sergio Nolazco",
   "author": "4c62fdc31c385890",
   "date": "2017-06-08T00:07:38Z",
   "subject": "Re: To exclude or not to exclude competitive models with negligible effects' covariates?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Just to add, discarding models with uninformative covariates and then using model averaging is actually mixing approaches (i.e., model selection and model averaging)?<br></div><div><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9tmVxAwgBgAJ",
   "from": "Eric Rexstad",
   "author": "f2122b34f1aa6e95",
   "date": "2017-06-08T08:10:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: [distance-sampling] To exclude or not to exclude competitive models with negligible effects' covariates?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n  \r\n    \r\n  \r\n  <div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\">\r\n    <p>Sergio</p>\r\n    <p>You appear to be doing a very thorough analysis of your data. \r\n      You will note that the point estimates of the 11 models you\r\n      present span a range 220-260; contrast that range with the\r\n      confidence interval from any single estimate and you will see the\r\n      point estimates are robust (insensitive) to choice of model.  That\r\n      is a common property of &quot;good&quot; distance sampling data.</p>\r\n    <p>Regarding your question of whether to remove some models before\r\n      performing model averaging; I would suggest &quot;no&quot;.  You\r\n      (presumably) created your candidate model set a priori.  Live with\r\n      that decision.  I accept that I have more uncertainty in my\r\n      estimates that I would wish.<br>\r\n    </p>\r\n    <p>As an aside, my personal preference (other analysts have other\r\n      preferences) is to not use adjustment terms when including\r\n      covariates in the detection function.  My belief is that\r\n      adjustment terms and covariates are performing similar tasks;\r\n      explaining patterns in detectability not explained by the key\r\n      function.<br>\r\n    </p></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\">\r\n    <br>\r\n    <div>On 07/06/2017 19:47, Sergio Nolazco\r\n      wrote:<br>\r\n    </div>\r\n    </div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\"><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n      \r\n      <div dir=\"ltr\"><b>Dear All,</b>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>Sometimes running distance sampling analyses for a same\r\n          dataset results in many competitive models (delta AIC &lt; 2).\r\n          Maybe this is a very common situation and one I am facing\r\n          right now. The problem is that at the end I am interest in\r\n          obtaining a single &quot;best&quot; abundance estimation and not a large\r\n          set of close values from many models.   </div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>In my case I got 11 models (including CDS and MCDS) with\r\n          delta AICc &lt; 2. All of my models with categorical\r\n          covariates other than distance did not improve the model fit\r\n          enough to overcome the penalization imposed by the AICc\r\n          calculation. From what I know, in this case the additional\r\n          covariates have a negligible effect. In this case, it is valid\r\n          or adequate to simply throw out those models and stay only\r\n          with the CDS models (in which perpendicular distance is the\r\n          only explanatory variable)? And then apply model averaging on\r\n          those CDS models to obtain density and population estimated\r\n          values? </div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>The other option I am thinking about is to apply model\r\n          averaging using all models (CDS and MCDS). However, some of my\r\n          MCDS models got large or undesirable coefficients of variation\r\n          (CV &gt; 25%) in relation to all other models. Consequently,\r\n          when applying model averaging using all models I get a much\r\n          larger variation and thus lower precision for my estimated\r\n          values of abundance in contrast to averaging only CDS models.\r\n                    </div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>Thanks in advance for any opinion or comment about this\r\n          matter, I am not completely sure on what option to choose or\r\n          if there is another &quot;best&quot; alternative. What concerns me most\r\n          is that between choosing one or other option there can be a\r\n          significant difference, not in my point estimates, but in\r\n          their precision.</div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>Here is a table of the summary of models (ordered by\r\n          increasing delta AICc). cov1 and cov2 are additional\r\n          covariates other than distance.<br>\r\n          <div>\r\n            <div><br>\r\n            </div>\r\n            <div>\r\n              <table style=\"text-align:left;border-collapse:collapse;width:462pt\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" width=\"615\" border=\"0\">\r\n                <colgroup><col style=\"width:92pt\" width=\"122\"> <col style=\"width:56pt\" width=\"75\"> <col style=\"width:55pt\" width=\"73\"> <col style=\"width:32pt\" width=\"43\"> <col style=\"width:40pt\" width=\"53\"> <col style=\"width:53pt\" width=\"71\"> <col style=\"width:32pt\" width=\"42\"> <col style=\"width:39pt\" width=\"52\"> <col style=\"width:63pt\" width=\"84\"> </colgroup><tbody>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:16.5pt\" height=\"22\">\r\n                    <td style=\"height:16.5pt;width:92pt\" height=\"22\" width=\"122\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">Covariate</span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:56pt\" width=\"75\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">Model</span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:55pt\" width=\"73\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">parameters</span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:32pt\" width=\"43\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">ΔAICc</span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:40pt\" width=\"53\">weights</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:53pt\" width=\"71\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\"><font>GOF Chi</font><font>-p</font></span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:32pt\" width=\"42\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">CV</span></td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:39pt\" width=\"52\">Density</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center;width:63pt\" width=\"84\"><span lang=\"PT-BR\">Estimate</span></td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">\r\n                    <td style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">Distance+cov1</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left\">Hnorm+pol</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">3</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.15</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.68</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">25.62</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.041</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">240</td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">\r\n                    <td style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">Distance+cov1</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left\">Hnorm</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">2</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.17</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.14</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.56</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">13.26</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.038</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">222</td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left;height:15pt\" height=\"20\">Distance</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left\">Hnorm+pol</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">2</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.37</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.12</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.74</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">13.28</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.040</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">238</td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left;height:15pt\" height=\"20\">Distance</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left\">Hnorm</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">1</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.60</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.11</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.62</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">13.17</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.037</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">220</td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" height=\"20\">\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left;height:15pt\" height=\"20\">Distance</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:left\">Hnorm+cos</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">2</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.80</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.10</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.76</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">15.16</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">0.042</td>\r\n                    <td style=\"text-align:center\">246</td>\r\n                  </tr>\r\n                  <tr style=\"height:15.0pt\" heig